Legal Counsel advised: 



{quote}In Portugal, the Public Prosecutor’s Office (Ministério Público) has the legal authority to request relevant information from public or private entities during a criminal investigation (inquérito). This power is granted under the *Portuguese Code of Criminal Procedure*.

* The Public Prosecutor is responsible for conducting the investigation (inquérito), assisted by the criminal police authorities, who act under its direction (Articles 53 and 263);
* The Public Prosecutor has the powers of judicial authority during the investigation (inquérito) and may carry out all acts and measures permitted by law to uncover the truth and ensure the enforcement of the law (Article 55);
* According to Article 267, the Public Prosecutor’s Office shall perform the acts and secure the evidence necessary to achieve the purposes referred to in Article 262(1), under certain terms and restrictions set out in the Code.
* According to Article 262, the investigation (inquérito) comprises all measures aimed at investigating the existence of a crime, determining its perpetrators and their responsibility, and discovering and gathering evidence in order to decide on the charges.{quote}



Long story short: Article 55 is the main reason they can do this (having powers of a judicial authority). Exception created and instructions added to [https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/19F9dMxdObEnLmTW4PazyKso7fM5c7SSkUwPsON2X1Ow/edit?gid=0#gid=0|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/19F9dMxdObEnLmTW4PazyKso7fM5c7SSkUwPsON2X1Ow/edit?gid=0#gid=0|smart-link]

